-- title:
--   Uniform bound on lattice points in a ball
-- statement:
--   Let $\mathbb{Z}^d \subseteq \mathbb{R}^d$ be the standard integer lattice, let $a \in \mathbb{R}^d$ be any centre and let $R \in \mathbb{R}$ be any radius. Then the number of lattice points in the open Euclidean ball of radius $R$ about $a$ satisfies
--   $$\#\bigl(B(a,R) \cap \mathbb{Z}^d\bigr) \;\le\; \bigl(2\lceil R\rceil + 1\bigr)^{d},$$
--   where $\lceil R \rceil$ is the least natural number at least $R$ (so $\lceil R\rceil = 0$ for $R \le 0$).
--
--   The essential feature is that the bound is **uniform in the centre**: it depends only on the radius and the dimension, not on where the ball sits. Bounds of this kind are the counting input for every multiplicity estimate, where one needs to know that each object can be reached only by boundedly many others, with a constant that does not degrade as the configuration moves around $\mathbb{R}^d$.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The cardinality is `Set.ncard`, which assigns the value $0$ to an infinite set; here the set is finite, so no junk value occurs. No hypothesis is placed on $R$: for $R \le 0$ the ball is empty and the bound reads $\#\emptyset \le 1$.
